   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.2. Increased institutional capacity of workers&#x2019; organizations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Federation of Trade Unions of the Republic of Kazakhstan (FPRK) made proposals to improve compliance with International Labour Standards and simplify labour dispute resolution in the Law of the Republic of Kazakhstan &quot;On Amendments and Additions to Certain Legislative Acts of the Republic of Kazakhstan to Simplify the Procedure for Resolving Labor Disputes and Conflicts&quot;.

The proposals were included in the Law that was adopted by the Parliament and signed by the President of the Kazakhstan on 15 February 2023. It addresses several gaps in the labour dispute resolution including organization of strike, procedure for organizing negotiations, strengthening of the institution of conciliation commissions, labour arbitration and the role of trade unions in resolving labour disputes. The law provides for amendments and additions to the Labour Code of the Republic of Kazakhstan and the Law of the Republic of Kazakhstan &quot;On Trade Unions&quot;. In accordance with the Law, the procedures for organizing strikes are simplified. The obligation of the employer to provide a meeting room is fixed. The decision to hold a strike can be made not by 2/3, but by a simple majority, while the quorum of the conference is also reduced. In addition, the terms of the period for consideration of claims are reduced. For example, the creation of a labour arbitration from 5 to 2 days, the adoption of a decision by a labour arbitration from 7 to 5 days. The concept of an hourly warning strike, which can be held during the arbitration period, has been introduced. The Law also provides for the introduction of representatives of the Tripartite Commissions on social partnership and regulation of social and labour relations into the composition of labour arbitration, which will make it possible to intensify the work of this institution. The ILO provided support through several seminars:
1. An online seminar on International Labour Standards (ILS) and presentation of best international practices on collective labour dispute resolution for representatives of Kazakhstan constituents members of the tripartite Dispute Resolution Working Group (December 2022).

2. A virtual seminar on ILS and the ILO supervisory mechanism for representatives of Kazakhstan constituents members of the Labour Legislation Reform Working Group (December 2022).

3. A seminar for the leaders and senior staff members of the Federation of Trade Unions of the Republic of Kazakhstan and the Kazakhstan Labour Confederation on the role of social dialogue and trade unions in promoting the ratification and compliance with international labour standards through effective use of ILO supervisory mechanism, including by addressing observations of the Committee of Experts (July 2022).</narrative>
   </description>
